WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.690
This video is going to be about the proof
of Bell's theorem, but I made another video

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.960
explaining the significance of Bell's theorem
and I think it would really helpful to watch

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.910
that first -- So if you haven't watched that
video then you can click this link to be taken

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:16.630
to it.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.990
So hopefully Nobody Who Hasn't Watched The
Other Video is here.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:26.000
Remember that what we want to prove today
is Bell's theorem that entangled particles

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.980
communicate to each other faster than light.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.210
Before I start on the details of the proof,
I'll explain the main idea.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:38.850
Last video I told you guys about something
called Spin, and some weird experimental facts

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.120
about it.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.830
I won't show you how, but it is possible to
explain Spin in the frame work of Quantum

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:45.830
Mechanics.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:50.310
However, say we didn't believe anything could
go faster than light.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.110
Then, because of EPR, quantum mechanics couldn't
be true.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:59.070
Therefore, we'd have to look for an alternative
theory where nothing ever acted faster than

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.070
light.

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.720
We'll call all the theories in this category
'local theories'.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.110
What this proof is going to show is that,
no matter how cleverly we try, we could never

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.930
come up with a local theory that can explain
spin.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.580
We'll do that by a proof by contradiction.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.100
In other worlds we start by believing it is
possible and see what happens.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.890
There's one particular experiment with spin
that we'll try explain.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.820
Let's say I have some source of electrons
with entangled spins.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.830
This means that if A and B are a pair, if
I choose to measure them both in the same

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.840
axis, one will be up and the other down.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.170
Now, A and B are sent off to two machines
very far from each other, which we will call

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.070
Alice and Bob.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:46.180
Alice and Bob can each measure the spin of
their electron in three different axes.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:52.120
These are the 3 axes and we choose them so
that they are 120 degrees apart from each

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.120
other.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.560
When A arrives at Alice, Alice randomly chooses
an axes to measure A's spin in, and Bob does

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.240
the same thing with B.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.190
But they do it independently from each other,
so Alice could measure spin around 2 and Bob

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.630
around 3 for example.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.250
If the machines measure their electron to
be spin up then they flash red, or if it's

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:15.010
spin down they flash white.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.150
Someone notes if they're the same colour or
not.

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.900
This experiment is repeated many times.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.760
The question is, what fraction of the time
will their colours be different?

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.010
The answer is actually half, and if you're
willing to take my word for that then you

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.670
can skip this part and move to the most interesting
part of the proof.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:38.780
If not, then stick around and we'll figure
out why that's the case, using the rules of

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.820
spin from last time.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.070
Ok, I'm assuming you've decided to stay.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:46.730
Let's say Alice measured around axis 1, and
happened to get spin down.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.050
Let's see how likely Bob is to measure spin
down as well.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.990
The reason I can just pick a particular case
like this is because all the other cases will

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.880
work out in the same way, to the same result.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.370
Well, one 1/3 of the time, Bob will choose
axis 1 as well, so since A and B are entangled,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:08.489
Bob's particle will definitely have spin up
in that direction so the colours don't match.

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:11.670
What about if Bob measured around axis 2?

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:16.280
Because A is down in the first axis, B must
be spin up in the first axis, so we just have

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.370
to figure out, how much of this spin is pointing
in the 2nd direction.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:25.700
Actually as you can see, this vector is partly
pointing in the negative direction of axis

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.790
2.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.069
What does this mean?

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:33.319
It means that if you measure the spin in axis
2, it is either going to be up or down, but

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:36.390
more likely down.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:42.040
So in this case A and B are more likely than
not going to have the same sp in and the colours

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.680
are more likely to be the same.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.459
What about axis 3?

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:49.489
This is a really similar story to 2.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:54.630
As you can see, this vector is pointing in
the negative direction of axis 3, so most

00:03:54.630 --> 00:04:00.880
of the time B will be down if it is measured
in axis 3, so again they A and B will most

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.300
likely have the same colour.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:08.769
let's review those 3 cases again:
If Bob is on axis 1, Alice and Bob definitely

00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:11.019
won't have the same colour.

00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:15.880
If Bob is on axis 2 or 3 though, more often
than not Alice and Bob will have the same

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:17.250
colour.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:23.410
It works out so that overall there's an exactly
50% chance that Alice and Bob have the same

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.949
colour and 50% chance they don't.

00:04:25.949 --> 00:04:31.110
Ok, so now, we're going to see if a local
theory could explain this result.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:37.279
Firstly let's think about what happens when
Alice and Bob happen to pick the same axis.

00:04:37.279 --> 00:04:41.050
A and B have to be opposite spins.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:45.719
We know that if nothing goes faster than light,
the only way this is can happen is if the

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:50.180
particles decided there spins while they were
in contact.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.580
We don't know how they decide who gets which
spin but the awesome thing about this argument

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.770
is that it doesn't matter.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:01.800
All that matters is that each particle A and
B have decided what there spins are in each

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.520
of the 3 directions before hand.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.669
There are 8 possibilities:

00:05:06.669 --> 00:05:11.990
This one is that A is spin up in axis 1 and
3 but spin down in axis 2.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:14.639
Then of course B is the opposite.

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:18.880
It would take a while if we had to go through
every single possibility, but luckily for

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:19.999
me we don't.

00:05:19.999 --> 00:05:25.960
We'll just look at the case were A is spin
up in axis 1 and 3 and spin down in 2 and

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.009
the case where A is spin up in every axis.

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:31.939
Why can we get away with this?

00:05:31.939 --> 00:05:36.439
Because we can get the other cases either
by swapping which particle we call A and B,

00:05:36.439 --> 00:05:40.930
which doesn't matter, or swapping the names
of the axes, which also doesn't matter.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.419
Convince yourself that that's true.

00:05:43.419 --> 00:05:47.599
Ok, so let's consider the case that A is all
spin up.

00:05:47.599 --> 00:05:52.469
If so what's the chance that Alice and Bob
will have the same colour?

00:05:52.469 --> 00:05:57.819
No matter which axes get picked A is always
up, and B is down, so they're always different

00:05:57.819 --> 00:06:00.809
spins whenever they adopt this plan.

00:06:00.809 --> 00:06:02.919
What about the other plan?

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:06.990
This is A's spins in each axis and underneath
is B's.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:12.649
Alice and Bob randomly pick an axes, and there
are a few possibilities for what happens:

00:06:12.649 --> 00:06:18.029
Alice could pick 1 then Bob could pick 1 as
well, so they are different colours

00:06:18.029 --> 00:06:23.139
Or he could pick 2 which means that they'd
have the same colour etc, and we just go through

00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:33.949
and tally up all 9 possibilities and find
that 5 out of the nine times they are different

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:35.430
colours.

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:40.699
So that means that whether or not A and B
picked a plan like this or like the previous

00:06:40.699 --> 00:06:44.339
type, they will always be more likely to have
the same colour.

00:06:44.339 --> 00:06:48.219
But as we already know, this isn't the right
result

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:53.279
So you see, local theories just don't have
the right tools to explain spin.

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:58.219
I think that's because spin is a crazy phenomena
that is nothing like the classical properties

00:06:58.219 --> 00:07:00.319
of matter that we're used to.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:05.430
On the hand any local theory is quite classical
because it assumes particles can't effect

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:11.119
each other instantly over distances which
really limits how weird it can be and in this

00:07:11.119 --> 00:07:15.330
case, its just too classical to explain something
like spin.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.089
So that's Bell's theorem.

00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:22.659
I promised to cover a couple of topics last
time so I'll work on that, but don't expect

00:07:22.659 --> 00:07:24.339
them too soon.

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:26.599
On that optimistic note, see you next time.

